00:33:26,785 --> 00:33:27,986
[SPEAKER_03]: And it will run out of oxygen.

382
00:33:29,928 --> 00:33:31,230
[SPEAKER_03]: Because oxygen comes from outside.

383
00:33:32,090 --> 00:33:33,532
[SPEAKER_03]: But there is no outside to this.

384
00:33:36,094 --> 00:33:36,915
[SPEAKER_03]: It cannot persist.

385
00:33:38,583 --> 00:33:45,245
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, exactly, you got rid of it, just in the right way, without you needing to claim against it.

386
00:33:49,866 --> 00:34:06,350
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's, you know, I was saying that in other talks a long time ago, you know, you can have your mind preoccupied with stuff, ideas and so on, but you can always discern peripherally that you just, in the midst of that, like, deeply engaging

387
00:34:07,545 --> 00:34:24,474
[SPEAKER_03]: Passionate involvement of your mental level, you find your body just there, scratching, oozing, sitting, breathing, this... this thing, this creature, oblivious to all your worries and projects and whatever else.

388
00:34:24,514 --> 00:34:26,034
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just... just there.

389
00:34:27,435 --> 00:34:35,019
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you overlook it, then you ignore it, and then you continue to prioritize your intentional level, of your engagements, of your spilling out, of your acting out.

390
00:34:36,692 --> 00:34:37,752
[SPEAKER_03]: And why is that possible?

391
00:34:38,073 --> 00:34:41,614
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you haven't trained yourself sufficiently enough in the right order.

392
00:34:42,855 --> 00:34:51,939
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you do, then even if you don't wish and want for the body to contain the passionate projects, it will.

393
00:34:53,239 --> 00:34:54,460
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's the order of things.

394
00:34:58,161 --> 00:35:06,285
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if you don't wish, oh, you know, may I stop regarding this mind, this mind, stop identifying with it,

395
00:35:07,039 --> 00:35:08,740
[SPEAKER_03]: Stop suffering and bring pressure on account of it.

396
00:35:08,800 --> 00:35:17,602
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you will stop, because you're containing it and you're removing the fuel for its wilderness, for its passion.

397
00:35:18,582 --> 00:35:22,083
[SPEAKER_03]: That fuel being nothing other than that wrong order, that it was the source of it.

398
00:35:25,284 --> 00:35:27,905
[SPEAKER_03]: Overstepping, misconceiving,

399
00:35:44,681 --> 00:35:52,444
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the practice of mindfulness of breathing, the practice of non-forgetfulness of the breath, is the practice of yoniso manasikāra.

400
00:35:56,246 --> 00:36:02,368
[SPEAKER_03]: Not directly attending to your breathing, but discerning that you already know you're breathing, even if you don't attend to it.

401
00:36:04,289 --> 00:36:04,869
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the key.

402
00:36:30,564 --> 00:36:33,266
[SPEAKER_03]: And what would you do if you get too involved with your breath?

403
00:36:38,289 --> 00:36:38,850
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that a problem?

404
00:36:44,914 --> 00:36:54,941
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd do the same thing.

405
00:36:55,702 --> 00:36:56,042
[SPEAKER_02]: Which one?

406
00:36:56,102 --> 00:36:57,343
[SPEAKER_02]: You would be outlining that.

407
00:36:58,788 --> 00:37:03,030
[SPEAKER_02]: Involvement, again, you would outline your body.

408
00:37:04,431 --> 00:37:14,796
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, but what I mean, if you get too involved with your breath, say, you are recollecting the knowledge of breathing correctly, but now you're feeling your lungs and energies and just overly attentive to it.

409
00:37:14,816 --> 00:37:27,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you have to basically just do the same like what you just said, in regard to any passionate intention or any... Well, you might not be passionate, but say you are overly focused on your breathing now.

410
00:37:28,458 --> 00:37:29,078
[SPEAKER_03]: Is that a problem?

411
00:37:29,358 --> 00:37:30,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it is a problem.

412
00:37:32,900 --> 00:37:33,760
[SPEAKER_03]: Go on.

413
00:37:33,820 --> 00:37:35,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Describe.

414
00:37:35,841 --> 00:37:40,683
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, you don't have to stop breathing in order to resolve the problem.

415
00:37:40,703 --> 00:37:43,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the problem is not that, like, you're breathing too much.

416
00:37:43,684 --> 00:37:52,907
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that you are, like, becoming overly involved, as you said, by either, like, I will try and do it like this, I will try and focus it on like that.

417
00:37:52,927 --> 00:37:54,968
[SPEAKER_00]: But you see, you can discern there's a difference.

418
00:37:55,508 --> 00:37:57,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't need to

419
00:37:58,242 --> 00:37:59,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Do all of that in order to breathe.

420
00:38:00,163 --> 00:38:00,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

421
00:38:00,403 --> 00:38:05,564
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're already breathing, like, normally, before you then start.

422
00:38:05,904 --> 00:38:06,284
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

423
00:38:06,364 --> 00:38:07,205
[SPEAKER_03]: So, we covered all that.

424
00:38:07,245 --> 00:38:10,186
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you do, but you do find yourself too focused.

425
00:38:10,206 --> 00:38:11,086
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how do you stop there?

426
00:38:11,266 --> 00:38:16,948
[SPEAKER_01]: The feeling is the counterpart to that becoming too absorbed.

427
00:38:16,988 --> 00:38:21,729
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a... to see

428
00:38:27,514 --> 00:38:37,221
[SPEAKER_01]: That pressure, then, I would say, feeling becomes more relevant as a way to... Well, you just keep it within the same.

429
00:38:37,621 --> 00:38:38,382
[SPEAKER_03]: Just breathing.

430
00:38:39,283 --> 00:38:40,203
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not complicated.

431
00:38:40,704 --> 00:38:46,007
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, it might work, what you're saying, but you're knowing you're breathing.

432
00:38:46,688 --> 00:38:51,952
[SPEAKER_03]: The next thing you know, you're just feeling the breath too much.

433
00:38:52,972 --> 00:38:53,913
[SPEAKER_03]: That's all you think about.

434
00:38:55,614 --> 00:38:56,435
[SPEAKER_03]: You're focused on it.

435
00:38:59,557 --> 00:39:00,057
[SPEAKER_03]: Remember what?

436
00:39:00,558 --> 00:39:00,898
[SPEAKER_03]: The breath.

437
00:39:01,078 --> 00:39:01,478
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

438
00:39:03,259 --> 00:39:08,261
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you stop overdoing it, in the same sense, you stop underdoing it.

439
00:39:09,442 --> 00:39:20,127
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, despite how deeply involved you might become with it, and absorbed, and like losing the perception of everything else, it's like, oh, but I forgot that I know that I'm already breathing, that the body is already breathing.

440
00:39:22,888 --> 00:39:24,109
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you remember the breathing.

441
00:39:26,050 --> 00:39:27,531
[SPEAKER_02]: I like this, how you're saying.

442
00:39:36,068 --> 00:39:42,554
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, you remember.

443
00:39:43,695 --> 00:39:46,497
[SPEAKER_03]: You remember that you already know you're breathing.

444
00:39:47,919 --> 00:39:49,200
[SPEAKER_03]: And that unfocuses you.

445
00:39:50,341 --> 00:39:53,283
[SPEAKER_03]: Because focusing means losing perspective.

446
00:39:55,085 --> 00:39:57,147
[SPEAKER_03]: Focusing means ayoniso.

447
00:39:58,167 --> 00:39:59,008
[SPEAKER_03]: However you spin it.

448
00:39:59,769 --> 00:40:00,990
[SPEAKER_02]: The blurring of the boundaries.

449
00:40:03,754 --> 00:40:04,235
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just gone.

450
00:40:04,335 --> 00:40:22,369
[SPEAKER_02]: But that unfocusing and, like I was saying, you mentioned outlining, confining, you know, unblurring those boundaries, and it's like, that's ānāpānasati, that's satipaṭṭhāna, that's sense-based contemplation, sense-objects, sense-consciousness, sense-content, feelings.

451
00:40:22,389 --> 00:40:25,711
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's like, all that is the same thing as ānāpānasati.

452
00:40:25,731 --> 00:40:30,575
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just outlining things, undermining, you know, outlining the domains, undermining the

453
00:40:33,446 --> 00:40:34,166
[SPEAKER_02]: By clarifying it.

454
00:40:34,186 --> 00:40:34,366
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

455
00:40:34,646 --> 00:40:35,907
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is yourself.

456
00:40:35,927 --> 00:40:36,767
[SPEAKER_02]: The blur is you.

457
00:40:36,787 --> 00:40:37,327
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

458
00:40:39,008 --> 00:40:40,088
[SPEAKER_03]: So, eh...

459
00:40:41,849 --> 00:40:48,071
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how should anyone understand the following instructions in anpanasati?

460
00:40:48,392 --> 00:40:58,575
[SPEAKER_03]: When you are supposed to breathe in long and short.

461
00:40:58,615 --> 00:41:02,217
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't that mean that you need to be more involved with the breath?

462
00:41:02,237 --> 00:41:03,117
[SPEAKER_03]: More focused on it?

463
00:41:07,773 --> 00:41:11,656
[SPEAKER_00]: No, not more involved, certainly not more focused.

464
00:41:12,716 --> 00:41:34,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, basically, first, like, if you're able to see clearly enough the intentionality of it, you can then sort of start exercising it further.

465
00:41:35,573 --> 00:41:36,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so to speak.

466
00:41:36,693 --> 00:41:37,654
[SPEAKER_03]: Without losing perspective.

467
00:41:37,674 --> 00:41:37,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

468
00:41:37,914 --> 00:41:38,734
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's the point.

469
00:41:38,914 --> 00:41:39,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

470
00:41:39,514 --> 00:41:49,916
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't start immediately by overly intentionally involving yourself with the breath, because you're already... well, the boundaries are already blurred.

471
00:41:50,897 --> 00:41:52,197
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to start through knowledge.

472
00:41:53,037 --> 00:41:56,458
[SPEAKER_03]: Knowledge of the breathing, that outlines the knowledge of your intention to breathe.

473
00:41:58,226 --> 00:41:58,726
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what I meant.

474
00:41:58,746 --> 00:42:01,528
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, those are not steps that you just do right off the bat.

475
00:42:01,928 --> 00:42:02,828
[SPEAKER_03]: I read it and I do it.

476
00:42:02,888 --> 00:42:05,750
[SPEAKER_03]: First thing I think of when I read that sutta, that's what that sutta means.

477
00:42:06,530 --> 00:42:07,911
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, good luck with that, really.

478
00:42:08,792 --> 00:42:10,312
[SPEAKER_03]: And that can be said for any sutta, actually.

479
00:42:11,793 --> 00:42:17,476
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like, no, you... Those are the aspects that you would have worked towards.

480
00:42:19,617 --> 00:42:26,841
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you outlined the intentionality of your breathing sufficiently enough, the physicality of your body,

481
00:42:27,616 --> 00:42:41,687
[SPEAKER_03]: If there is the container of anything, any subsequent mind state or intention or whatever, is second to it, within it, then, yes, you can push that intentionality further without blurring the boundaries.

482
00:42:41,867 --> 00:42:42,468
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the point.

483
00:42:44,529 --> 00:42:57,259
[SPEAKER_03]: Because he still knowingly breathes in long and knowingly breathes out long, knowingly, while breathing in, feels the body.

484
00:42:58,605 --> 00:42:59,646
[SPEAKER_03]: Discerns the mind.

485
00:43:01,006 --> 00:43:02,187
[SPEAKER_03]: Contemplates impermanence.

486
00:43:04,288 --> 00:43:04,649
[SPEAKER_03]: But why?

487
00:43:04,949 --> 00:43:08,131
[SPEAKER_03]: Why can't you just feel the body, discern the mind, contemplate impermanence directly?

488
00:43:08,151 --> 00:43:14,054
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, you can, but why is this a recommended way?

489
00:43:18,217 --> 00:43:26,362
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, eh... the impermanence and the body that you'll be recollecting is your own idea.

490
00:43:27,385 --> 00:43:28,646
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's the risk of it, yeah.

491
00:43:29,826 --> 00:43:36,709
[SPEAKER_03]: But that is rooted in the same, basically.

492
00:43:37,190 --> 00:43:41,051
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the cause of that would be the reason why you should be doing ānāpānasati.

493
00:43:41,171 --> 00:43:48,775
[SPEAKER_03]: So, still doesn't ask me my question as to why you should be contemplating those things through satipaṭṭhānas, as opposed to just do it yourself.

494
00:43:49,435 --> 00:43:55,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, okay, so, I think it's about basically balancing, like what you said in the beginning,

495
00:43:56,172 --> 00:44:00,155
[SPEAKER_00]: There's... you can kind of go too far in the direction of always just the body doing it.

496
00:44:00,435 --> 00:44:03,397
[SPEAKER_00]: And now I'm just, like, observing it, kind of, from the outside, almost.

497
00:44:04,258 --> 00:44:13,124
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can go too far in the direction of, like, I'm gonna be fully the one doing it, you know, controlling everything, and of it.

498
00:44:14,645 --> 00:44:21,750
[SPEAKER_00]: So... so it's about, basically, not doing too much of either.

499
00:44:22,591 --> 00:44:24,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Not going too much in either direction.

500
00:44:24,312 --> 00:44:25,273
[SPEAKER_00]: But... so...

501
00:44:25,793 --> 00:44:43,803
[SPEAKER_00]: If you just kind of stay only on the level of knowledge of the breath, which you... is valid, and can already take you quite far, there's a risk of the... there is a risk of the intentionality aspect of it not become... not being clear enough, I would say.

502
00:44:44,083 --> 00:44:49,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's just going to be kind of a recollection of something that you're not doing deliberately.

503
00:44:50,452 --> 00:44:54,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Or it's not like, it's a kind of, oh yeah, sure, I know about it, but it's not...

504
00:44:54,215 --> 00:44:55,395
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, that's what I'm asking, though.

505
00:44:55,535 --> 00:44:55,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

506
00:44:56,396 --> 00:44:57,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what are you asking?

507
00:44:57,517 --> 00:45:01,259
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what I'm asking is, you can directly contemplate impermanence.

508
00:45:01,680 --> 00:45:02,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, it is.

509
00:45:02,360 --> 00:45:10,305
[SPEAKER_03]: You can directly contemplate your mind, or you can do it through ānāpānasati, or satipaṭṭhāna.

510
00:45:11,046 --> 00:45:17,350
[SPEAKER_03]: And, given the way the Buddha was talking about it, it seems to indicate that it's better to do it through ānāpānasati or through a satipaṭṭhāna

511
00:45:21,293 --> 00:45:23,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it makes those contemplations concrete.

512
00:45:24,274 --> 00:45:25,575
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's evident enough.

513
00:45:25,915 --> 00:45:26,775
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why it's better.

514
00:45:27,155 --> 00:45:28,696
[SPEAKER_03]: But that still doesn't explain the difference.

515
00:45:32,598 --> 00:45:34,798
[SPEAKER_03]: Why are these concrete and these are not so concrete?

516
00:45:35,899 --> 00:45:41,521
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the factor that determines the concreteness of a contemplation?

517
00:45:46,323 --> 00:45:47,944
[SPEAKER_03]: Movement.

518
00:45:50,500 --> 00:45:52,681
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the single factor that follows through?

519
00:45:54,282 --> 00:45:56,163
[SPEAKER_03]: We already said it a few times now.

520
00:45:56,483 --> 00:45:59,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're developing it through yonisonmanasikāra.

521
00:45:59,524 --> 00:46:00,785
[SPEAKER_03]: Because of yonisonmanasikāra.

522
00:46:02,405 --> 00:46:03,526
[SPEAKER_03]: Because of yonisonmanasikāra.

523
00:46:04,226 --> 00:46:04,747
[SPEAKER_03]: Full stop.

524
00:46:05,887 --> 00:46:10,609
[SPEAKER_03]: Because yonisonmanasikāra can become something you take for granted.

525
00:46:11,049 --> 00:46:12,110
[SPEAKER_03]: You're familiar with it.

526
00:46:12,750 --> 00:46:15,451
[SPEAKER_03]: You're developed in it to some extent.

527
00:46:17,052 --> 00:46:19,233
[SPEAKER_03]: And yes, when you attend to things directly,

528
00:46:20,233 --> 00:46:32,821
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, you are attending those things through yoniso manasikāra, to some extent, but not fully, because if you were, well, you would be an arahant.

529
00:46:35,142 --> 00:46:43,627
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, although you already know the principle of yoniso manasikāra inside out, you haven't fully trained in it yourself.

530
00:46:44,728 --> 00:46:45,328
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you know?

531
00:46:45,448 --> 00:46:46,969
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you're not an arahant yet.

532
00:46:49,722 --> 00:46:56,383
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's exactly why ānāpānasati was instructed to sotāpana, sakadāgāmī, anāgāmīs, those who still had some work to do.

533
00:46:58,183 --> 00:46:59,784
[SPEAKER_03]: That's exactly why they could understand it.

534
00:47:03,584 --> 00:47:10,486
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, okay, yeah, of course, I absolutely have mastered the knowledge of yoniso manasikāra inside out.

535
00:47:10,566 --> 00:47:12,066
[SPEAKER_03]: I can explain it in hundreds of ways.

536
00:47:12,726 --> 00:47:14,506
[SPEAKER_03]: But, am I an anahant yet?

537
00:47:15,266 --> 00:47:15,446
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

538
00:47:15,706 --> 00:47:19,167
[SPEAKER_03]: Means that yoniso manasikāra has not been thoroughly developed.

539
00:47:20,781 --> 00:47:23,002
[SPEAKER_03]: I haven't trained in it sufficiently enough.

540
00:47:23,223 --> 00:47:27,065
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly what you would do through satipaṭṭhāna and ānāpānasati, whether you want it or not.

541
00:47:37,611 --> 00:47:50,319
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it will prevent you, or it will maintain a degree of that... a necessary degree of that indirectness,

542
00:47:50,872 --> 00:48:04,513
[SPEAKER_03]: Indirectedness to your contemplation of impermanence, to your... through your discerning and communicating with your citta and and so on, that you can overlook or neglect.

543
00:48:33,793 --> 00:48:35,954
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, so it anchors it, essentially.

544
00:48:45,460 --> 00:49:03,150
[SPEAKER_03]: So, through recollectiveness, non-forgetfulness of the knowledge of your body breathing here and now, everything that's there, with it, you regard as impermanent.

545
00:49:06,492 --> 00:49:11,535
[SPEAKER_03]: And the tendency to, if you were to have it, to...

546
00:49:12,235 --> 00:49:17,338
[SPEAKER_03]: But let me now go into those things and really see them as impermanent.

547
00:49:18,398 --> 00:49:19,098
[SPEAKER_03]: That's overdoing.

548
00:49:20,639 --> 00:49:22,040
[SPEAKER_03]: That's taking away from oneself.

549
00:49:24,821 --> 00:49:30,284
[SPEAKER_03]: So, instead of giving in to that tendency to go and overdo it, you stay anchored.

550
00:49:31,785 --> 00:49:33,506
[SPEAKER_03]: You stay with the right reference point.

551
00:49:34,606 --> 00:49:36,167
[SPEAKER_02]: Peripheral knowledge of the body breathing.

552
00:49:38,107 --> 00:49:45,452
[SPEAKER_03]: And just allow the perception of impermanence in regard to those things to that extent that they are revealed.

553
00:49:46,033 --> 00:49:47,153
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't need to reveal them all.

554
00:49:49,295 --> 00:49:50,016
[SPEAKER_03]: And train in that.

555
00:49:54,259 --> 00:50:00,143
[SPEAKER_03]: Same in regard to the feelings, in regard to the state of the animal, the citta.

556
00:50:01,764 --> 00:50:02,224
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like this.

557
00:50:02,344 --> 00:50:03,225
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't need to fix it.

558
00:50:03,305 --> 00:50:04,086
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't need to change it.

559
00:50:04,726 --> 00:50:05,507
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, now it's like this.

560
00:50:05,647 --> 00:50:05,967
[SPEAKER_03]: Again,

561
00:50:06,578 --> 00:50:07,418
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't need to fix it.

562
00:50:07,539 --> 00:50:08,439
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't need to change it.

563
00:50:09,320 --> 00:50:10,960
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't need to do anything on account of it.

564
00:50:12,641 --> 00:50:15,663
[SPEAKER_03]: And that becomes clearer and clearer if you remain anchored.

565
00:50:17,424 --> 00:50:19,505
[SPEAKER_03]: If you remain seeing it indirectly.

